# Real-interrupt-taking corpus for the lockstep harness's mutant kill
# matrix -- unlike act.list/fw.list/random (qvgen.py's own corpus never
# enables mstatus.MIE, so --irq is a reserved no-op there), these
# programs actually take real interrupts. Deliberately NOT part of
# --corpus all's aggregate -- run with --irq-mode 2 explicitly, since
# every other corpus entry expects the default irq_mode=0.
#
# MAX_RETIRE-based (not tohost), deliberately -- see repro_irq_stall.s's
# own header for why a tohost-based run on a mutant that only skews
# TIMING (not WHICH instruction retires) can grow its retirement count
# without bound instead of ever cleanly finishing PASS or FAIL. A fixed
# MAX_RETIRE sidesteps that: it stops both sides after a fixed amount of
# real progress and compares final architectural state directly (via
# lockstep_tb.sv's own check_final_state), which is what actually
# proves this class of mutant caught -- confirmed empirically: QV_MUTANT
# B=5 (interrupt sampled one retirement late) FAILs here with a real
# final-register mismatch after a clean 1000-retirement RVFI stream;
# QV_MUTANT_B=0 PASSes cleanly at the same MAX_RETIRE.
#
# target | max_retire (hex file is always verification/lockstep/<target>.hex,
# built via riscv64-unknown-elf-as/ld/objcopy against gen/link.ld,
# matching repro_irq_stall.s's own documented build commands)
repro_irq_stall|1000
